                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "NOTE 3 Some applications could accept a low amount of entropy in the random number while others could require a very high amount of entropy. It is left up to the different applications/features/functions to define the amount of entropy needed for those particular applications/features/functions.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                473,
                1120,
                547
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    473.0
                ],
                [
                    1120.0,
                    473.0
                ],
                [
                    1120.0,
                    547.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    547.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "NOTE 4 As mentioned earlier, NRBGs can take some time to build entropy. If the seed for the DRBG is generated by a NRBG, there can be a need to either wait for the NRBG to build enough entropy before requesting the NRBG to generate the random number (seed) or the random number could be generated and securely stored at a time when the NRBG is known to have a good entropy and using that random number to re-seed/re-initialize the DRBG when the need to re-seed/re-initialize the DRBG arrives/occurs.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                567,
                1121,
                688
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
